= Las compañeras

They have been here the whole time, and the movement has managed, with the tenacity it usually reserves for the enemy, not to see them: the women who carry the messages precisely because the checkpoints wave them through, who keep the safe houses that are the only reason the column has anywhere to be safe, who nurse the wounded and bury the dead and, increasingly, who ask why the rifle is always handed to a man. Some are already fighters. One of them, in the Guerrero columns, is quietly the best tactician the movement has, and everyone knows it, and no one has given her a command.

The question surfaces the way it always does — as a demand that sounds like an accusation because it is one: *is this a revolution for the compañeras, or a revolution the compañeras are asked to serve and then wait for?* The columns are watching how the leadership answers. So, in a different way, is history.

- @compas_mando: Give them command. The best cadre leads, and if that offends the men who assumed the sierra was theirs, the offense is overdue.
- @compas_doble: Value the work, defer the question — the war is not the moment to fight two fronts; the compañeras' liberation waits for the people's.
- @compas_ignorar: Let it lie. The column has enough arguments; this one can keep.

@compas_mando
choose-if: disciplina >= 40
unavailable-subtitle: Overturning the sierra's oldest assumption takes a discipline the columns do not have; the men would read a woman's command as one more thing falling apart.
on-arrival: compas_armadas = 2; moral_columnas += 4; disciplina += 3; pres_guerrero += 2; sup_students += 2; feminismo += 6; leyenda += 1

The comandanta takes her column, and the sierra does not end. What ends is a fiction — that the woman who could out-plan every man in the camp should keep making the coffee while they lost the arguments she would have won. Her operations are cleaner because she was never allowed the luxury of improvising; her column is tighter because it was assembled by someone who had to earn every scrap of the authority the men were handed. The corridos, catching up late, will give her a verse. The movement gave her, at last, the thing it owed her: the command she had already been exercising without the title.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@compas_doble
on-arrival: compas_armadas = 1; moral_columnas += 1; feminismo += 1; sup_students -= 1

The formula is old and the compañeras have heard it in every language the left speaks: first the revolution, then your revolution; carry the water now, and liberation will be distributed afterward, in an order to be determined by the people currently holding the rifles. They keep the safe houses. They keep the messages moving. They keep, also, a private ledger of the promise deferred, and when this war ends — however it ends — some of them will take what the sierra taught them into a different fight, the one the left kept telling them to postpone, and win it without the men who asked them to wait.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@compas_ignorar
on-arrival: feminismo -= 2; disciplina -= 1

The leadership has wars enough and files the question under *not now*, which the compañeras correctly translate as *not from you*. The work continues because they continue it; the resentment continues because it is earned. Nothing visibly changes, which is the problem — a movement that cannot see half of itself is a movement navigating with half a map, and the blindness it practices at home is the blindness that will one day walk an informer past a checkpoint no one thought to have a woman watch.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@la_companera_caida
title: La compañera caída
